Bos JD, Middelkoop E (1998) Differences in cellular infiltrate and extracellular matrix of chronic diabetic and venous ulcers vs acute wounds. J Invest Dermatol 111:850&#x02013;857 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9804349</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR14"><label>14.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wetzler</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Kampfer</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Stallmeyer</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Pfeilschifter</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Frank</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Large and sustained induction of chemokines during impaired wound healing in the genetically diabetic mouse: prolonged persistence of neutrophils and macrophages during the late phase of repair</article-title><source>J Invest Dermatol</source><year>2000</year><volume>115</volume><fpage>245</fpage><lpage>253</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1046/j.1523-1747.2000.00029.x</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wetzler C, Kampfer H, Stallmeyer B, Pfeilschifter J, Frank S (2000) Large and sustained induction of chemokines during impaired wound healing in the genetically diabetic mouse: prolonged persistence of neutrophils and macrophages during the late phase of repair. J Invest Dermatol 115:245&#x02013;253 <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10951242</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR15"><label>15.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lobmann</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Schultz</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Lehnert</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Proteases and the diabetic foot syndrome: mechanisms and therapeutic implications</article-title><source>Diabetes Care</source><year>2005